WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.120
Gradient checking is a technique that's helped me save tons of

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.560
time and helped me find

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:08.120
bugs in my implementations of backpropagation many times.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.960
Let's see how you could use it too to debug or to

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.480
verify that your implementation of backprop is correct.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:17.240
So your new network will have some set of parameters,

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:20.600
w1, b1, and so on up to wl, bl.

00:00:20.600 --> 00:00:22.400
So to implement gradient checking,

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.760
the first thing you should do is take all your parameters and

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.440
reshape them into a giant vector theta.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.440
So what you should do is take w,

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.640
which is a matrix, and reshape it into a vector.

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.700
You'll take all of these w's and reshape them into vectors,

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:42.400
and then concatenate all of these things so that you have

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.680
a giant vector theta, giant vector parameters theta.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.440
So instead of a cost function,

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:53.120
j being a function of the w's and b's,

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.600
you will now have the cost function j

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.080
being just a function of theta.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.040
Next, with w and b ordered the same way,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.760
you can also take dw1, db1, and so on,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.280
and reshape them into big giant vector d theta

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:12.120
of the same dimension as theta.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.480
So same as before, reshape dw1 into the matrix,

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:18.360
db1 is already a vector, reshape dwl,

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.120
all the dw's which are matrices.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.640
Remember, dw1 has the same dimension as w1,

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.920
db1 has the same dimension as b1.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:31.240
So with the same reshaping and concatenation operation,

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.880
you can then reshape all of these derivatives

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.360
into a giant vector d theta,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.640
which is the same dimension as theta.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:43.560
So the question is now, is d theta the gradient

00:01:43.560 --> 00:01:47.200
or the slope of the cost function j?

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.400
So here's how you implement gradient checking,

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.600
and often abbreviate gradient checking to grad check.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:55.480
So first, remember that j is now a function

00:01:55.480 --> 00:01:58.280
of the giant parameter theta.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:01:59.600
So you can also expand this out,

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:03.040
the j is a function of theta one, theta two,

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.160
theta three, and so on.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.120
Whatever is the dimension of this giant

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:11.360
parameter vector theta.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.640
So to implement gradient check,

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.840
what you're going to do is implement a loop

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.520
so that for each i, so for each component of theta,

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:25.520
let's compute d theta approx i to b.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.160
And we're gonna take a two-sided difference.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.000
So we're gonna take j of theta,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.320
theta one, theta two, up to theta i.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.240
And we're gonna nudge theta i to add epsilon to this.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:41.240
So just increase theta i by epsilon

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:42.840
and keep everything else the same.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.360
And because it's taking a two-sided difference,

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.240
we're going to do the same on the other side

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:51.440
with theta i but now minus epsilon.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.400
And then all of the other elements of theta are left alone.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:59.400
And then we'll take this and we divide it by two theta.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:02.400
And what we saw from the previous video

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.400
is that this should be approximately equal to d theta i,

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.120
which is supposed to be the partial derivative of j

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:16.840
with respect to, I guess, theta i,

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:21.240
if d theta i is the derivative of the cos function j.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.380
So what you're gonna do is you're gonna compute this

00:03:23.380 --> 00:03:25.040
for every value of i.

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.320
And at the end, you now end up with two vectors.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:32.280
You end up with this d theta approx.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.000
And this is gonna be the same dimension as d theta.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.560
And both of these are in turn the same dimension as theta.

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.960
And what you wanna do is check if these vectors

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:44.040
are approximately equal to each other.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:47.520
So in detail, well, how do you define whether or not

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:50.840
two vectors are really reasonably close to each other?

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:52.720
What I do is the following.

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.980
I would compute the Euclidean distance

00:03:55.980 --> 00:03:59.800
between these two vectors, d theta approx minus d theta.

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:02.120
So just the L2 norm of this.

00:04:02.120 --> 00:04:03.520
Notice there's no square on top.

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:05.680
So this is the sum of squares of elements

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.720
of the differences and then you take a square root

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:09.920
to actually get the Euclidean distance.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:13.080
And then just to normalize by the length

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:18.160
of these vectors, I divide by d theta approx plus d theta.

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:22.580
Just take the Euclidean lengths of these vectors.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:25.520
And the role for the denominator is just in case

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.480
any of these vectors are really small or really large,

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:32.800
the denominator turns this formula into a ratio.

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:34.660
So when you implement this in practice,

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:38.080
I use epsilon equals maybe 10 to the minus seven.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.000
So you have one e minus seven.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.960
And with this range of epsilon,

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:45.200
if you find that this formula gives you a value

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.440
like 10 to the minus seven or smaller, then that's great.

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.040
It means that your derivative approximation

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:53.440
is very likely correct.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.220
This is just very small value.

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:58.520
If it's maybe on the range of 10 to the minus five,

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:00.680
I would take a careful look.

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:02.240
Maybe this is okay.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.120
But I might double check the components of this vector

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.840
and make sure that none of the components are too large.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.160
And if some of the components of this difference

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.760
are very large, then maybe you have a bug somewhere.

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:15.560
And if this formula on the left is on the order

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:18.200
of 10 to the minus three, then I would worry.

00:05:18.200 --> 00:05:20.200
I would be much more concerned that maybe

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:21.880
there's a bug somewhere.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:23.640
But you should really be getting values

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:25.200
much smaller than 10 to the minus three.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:27.600
Or if it's any bigger than 10 to the minus three,

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.720
then I would be quite concerned.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:32.880
I would seriously worry about whether or not there's a bug.

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.520
And I would then, you should then look at

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.480
the individual components of theta to see

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:42.520
if there's a specific value of i for which

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:45.880
d theta procs i is very different from d theta i

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:47.680
and use that to try to track down

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.920
whether or not some of your derivative computations

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:50.920
might be incorrect.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.640
And after some amount of debugging,

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:56.360
if finally it ends up being this kind of very small value,

00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:59.680
then you probably have a correct implementation.

00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:01.280
So when implementing neural network,

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:03.760
what often happens is I'll implement forward prop,

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:05.800
implement back prop, and then I might find that

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:08.680
this rat check gives a relatively big value.

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:10.760
And then I would suspect that it must be a bug,

00:06:10.760 --> 00:06:14.120
go in, debug, debug, debug, and after debugging for a while,

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:16.680
if I find that it poshes this rat check with a small value,

00:06:16.680 --> 00:06:18.600
then you know, you can be much more confident

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:20.000
that it's then correct.

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:22.320
So you now know how gradient checking works.

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:23.720
This has helped me find lots of bugs

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:25.480
in my implementations with neural nets,

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:27.240
and I hope it'll help you too.

00:06:27.240 --> 00:06:29.160
In the next video, I want to share with you

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:32.400
some tips or some notes on how to actually implement

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:33.400
gradient checking.

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:34.840
Let's go on to the next video.
